## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of human society, the increase of population and environmental pollution are more and more serious. In order to realize the sustainable development of our society, various clean and renewable energy are attracted more attention and high-speed developed in the past few decades [1–3]. Meanwhile, the energy storage device plays an indispensable role in fabricating sustainable energy conversion and storage systems [4,5]. Supercapacitors (SCs, also commonly named as electrochemical capacitors or ultra-capacitors) are widely explored for energy storage application and possess some very attractive properties, such as higher power density, fast charge-discharge time, long cycle life, wide thermal operating range, and lower maintenance cost [6–9], which makes them exhibit promising application in many fields, such as utilization in supplement to batteries, for frequency regulation in smart grids, stand-by power systems, hybrid electric vehicles, etc [10–12].
SCs are generally classified into two types based on two different charge storage mechanism: electrical double layer supercapacitors (EDLCs) and pseudo-supercapacitors [10,13]. In EDLC, the charges are separately stored in the interface between electrode and electrolyte based on the electrical double layer theory. The electrode materials mainly contain carbon materials, such as graphene, CNT, mesoporous carbon material, etc [14–16]. The capacitance of EDLC strongly depends on the specific surface area of the carbon materials. Different from EDLC, the pseudo-supercapacitors reveal a greater amount of capacitance than the EDLC, owing to the fast and reversible redox reactions or Faradic charge transfer at the surface of electroactive sites, the pseudo-supercapacitors are always obtained through transition metal oxides [9,17], conducting polymers [18,19] or metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [20– 24]. MOFs are a kind of porous materials and have attracted intense attention during the past two decades, these materials are crystalline material assembled by central metal ions and organic linkers via strong coordinative bonds [25]. MOFs have been demonstrated to be useful for electrochemical energy storage and are considered as the most promising electrode material candidate for SCs due to their remarkable surface area, tunable pore size, controllable micro-pore structure and special structures with potential pseudo-capacitive redox centers [9,20,21,26]. Numerous efforts have been devoted to design different well-controlled structured MOFs for improving the capacitance and cycling stability. For example, Co-MOFs constructed with three different molecular lengths dicarboxylic acids were fabricated and utilized as the supercapacitor electrode, which can exhibit a highest specific capacitance of $179.2\:\mathrm{F/g}$ in alkaline solution [27]. Other structure of Co-MOF was also explored as a promising material for supercapacitors, which exhibits a good specific capacitance up to $206.76\:\mathrm{F}/$ g [28]. Recently, nickel based MOFs have been proved to have intense application prospect for supercapacitors. For instance, Kong’s group successfully synthesized a nickel based MOF using trimesic acid and nickel ions in mixture solution by a facile hydrothermal reaction, the Ni-MOF exhibited high specific capacitance of $726\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ [29]. The constructed hierarchical MOF structure is benefit to improve the specific capacitance and rate capability. Liu’s group successfully fabricated a novel pillared Ni-MOF using two different linker by hydrothermal method for supercapacitor and the pillared Ni-MOF showed specific capacitances of 552 and $438\:\mathrm{F/g}$ at current densities of 1 and $20\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ [30]. In order to achieve higher specific capacitances using MOFs materials, various layer structure MOFs have been developed and used as supercapacitor materials [31–33]. Pang’s group successfully synthesized a novel accordion-like Ni-MOF superstructure after ultra-sonicating the bulk Ni-MOF, the accordion-like Ni-MOF was used as electrode material in supercapacitor, and exhibited a high capacitance of $988\mathrm{F/g}$ and ${823}\mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $1.4\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ and $7.0\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ [32]. Meanwhile, Wei’s group prepared a layered structure Ni-MOF via one-step hydrothermal method using the $p$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid as organic linker and used as electrode material in a supercapacitor. This layered material exhibited a large capacitance of $1127\mathrm{F/g}$ and $668\ \mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of 0.5 and $10{\mathsf{A}}/{\mathsf{g}},$ respectively [33]. These unique Ni-MOF materials exhibit both high electrochemical performance and good rate capability. In addition, nanosheets assembled spherical nanocages were fabricated using ZIF-67 as the template by two step reaction [34,35], the nanosheets stacked structure exhibited high electrochemical property, the specific capacitance could reach to $1203\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ at current density of $1\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . Thus fabrication of hierarchical structure of MOFs constructed with nanosheets are an important strategy to improve both the electrochemical performance and good rate capability for directly using the MOFs as electrode materials in supercapacitor.
In this study, a uniform hierarchical porous Ni-MOF was fabricated using a facile hydrothermal reaction by adjusting the ratio of trimesic acid and nickel ion. The novel hierarchical porous NiMOF was constructed with nanosheets, the porous structure exhibited highest specific area. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF also possesses an outstanding specific capacitance of $1057\mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $1\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g},$ which also exhibits good rate capability of $63.4\%$ $(649\mathrm{F/g})$ even up to $30{\mathrm{A}}/{\mathrm{g}}{\mathrm{.}}$ . In addition, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF keeps $70\%$ of its original value up to 2 500 chargedischarge cycles at the current density of $10\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . Furthermore, ASCs were assembled based on hierarchical porous Ni-MOF and activated carbon (AC), the ASCs reveal specific capacitance of 87 $\mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $0.5\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}.$ and exhibit high energy density of $21.05\mathrm{Wh/kg}$ and power density of $6.03\mathrm{~kW/kg}$ . Additionally, the tandem ASCs can light up a red LED.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Trimesic acid and nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. N, NDimethylformamide (DMF) was received from Guangfu Chemical Reagent Co., used as received without further treatment.
## 2.2. Fabrication of hierarchical porous metal-organic framework (NiMOF)
In a typical procedure [36], trimesic acid $(0.0525\mathrm{g},0.25\mathrm{mmol}$ ) and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $\cdot0.4362{\mathrm{g}}$ 1.5 mmol) were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of N,N-dimethylformamide DMF with stirring at room temperature. After stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ , the obtained light green solution was then transformed into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the precipitate was centrifuged and washed with DMF and ethanol several times. Finally, the obtained product was dried in vacuum oven for $24\mathrm{h}$ , and the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF (Ni-MOF 1–6) was obtained. Herein, mole ratio of trimesic acid: $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ was set to 1:6, 1:4, 1:2, and 1:1, respectively, and the corresponding materials were denoted as Ni-MOF 1–6, Ni-MOF 1–4, Ni-MOF 1–2, and NiMOF 1–1, respectively.
## 2.3. Materials characterization
The morphologies of Ni-MOFs were investigated by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-1200 EX/S) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S-4800, Japan). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were characterized by a Shimadzu XRD-6000 Xray diffractometer (Shimadzu Corp., Kyoto, Japan) from $5^{\circ}$ to $80^{\circ}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was measured by a PHI5702 instrument using the C1s line at $284.8\mathrm{eV}$ as the binding energy reference. The Raman spectrums of Ni-MOFs were obtained from 3200 to $100\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ on the Thermo Scientific DXR Raman
Microscope using a wavelength of $633\mathrm{\nm\as}$ the laser source. The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy analysis was characterized by Bruker IFS $66~\mathrm{v/s}$ infrared spectrometer (Bruker, Karlsruhe, Germany) in the range of $400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}{-}4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results were measured with a
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
The working electrodes were prepared by dispersing the active electrode material $(70\%)$ , acetylene black $(20\%)$ and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) $(10\%)$ in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP). The obtained slurry was coated onto a slice of carbon current collector ( $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ area and $1\mathrm{mm}$ thick) and dried at $80^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mass of the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF is about $1.7\mathrm{mg}$ . The electrochemical measurements was carried out on a $\mathsf{C H I~660E}$ potentiostat electrochemical workstation. The cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge-discharge tests, and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of the individual working electrode were performed with a three-electrode cell in 3 M KOH aqueous solution, in which platinum foil and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) were used as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured in the frequency range of $10^{5}–0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ . To further investigate the electrochemical performance in two-electrode, the ASC based on Ni-MOF 1–6//AC was assembled, where Ni-MOF 1–6,
AC, and cellulose separator (NKK) served as positive electrode, negative electrode and separator, respectively. The mass of the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF and AC is about $1.7\mathrm{mg}$ and $2.4\:\mathrm{mg}$ .
## 3. Result and discussion
Hierarchical porous nickel based metal-organic framework (NiMOF) was fabricated by a facile one-step hydrothermal approach with the existence of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and trimesic acid. A schematic illustration of the fabrication process of hierarchical porous Ni-MOF is shown schematically in Scheme 1. Morphologies of the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF were investigated by TEM (Fig. 1) and SEM (Fig. 2). It can be clearly observed that the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF (NiMOF 1–6) is constructed by uniform cavity and numerous of nanosheets which has a relatively smooth surface with an average thickness of $10\mathrm{nm}$ according to TEM image (Fig. 1d) and SEM image (Fig. 2c). Otherwise, various morphologies of the Ni-MOFs can be fabricated through adjusting the mole ratio of the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and trimesic acid. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF was obtained, when the mole ratio of the trimesic acid to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ was set to 1:6. In the formation process, excessive amounts of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ existed in the precursor solution. In the initial stage, the Ni-MOF was formed.
Then, the Ni-MOF was etched by excessive amounts of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , the NiMOF nanosheets were simultaneously formed to fabricate hierarchical porous structure.[34,37] When the mole ratio of the trimesic acid to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ was 1:6, all of the Ni-MOF can transformed to hierarchical porous structure. However, when the mole ratio of trimesic acid and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ increased to 1:4, parts of the spherical structure of Ni-MOF were formed, it exhibited that the Ni-MOF wasn’t etched completely by $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ . As shown in Figs. $_{1\mathrm{e-g}}$ and $2\mathsf{d}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{f},$ , it was clearly observed that the mole ratio was higher, more spherical structure of Ni-MOF was obtained. Obviously, when the mole ratio increased to 1:1, the Ni-MOF presented all of the spherical structure with smooth surface.
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) was further used to investigate the structure of the Ni-MOFs. As shown in Fig. 3, the XRD pattern displays that the Ni-MOF 1–1 presents two distinct broad diffraction peaks standing at $10^{\circ}$ and $22^{\circ}$ , which correspond to the (1 0 0) and (1 0 1) planes, respectively [38]. In addition, the most prominent peak at $7^{\circ}$ for Ni-MOF 1–6 is corresponding to the (0 1 1) plane, which reveals the improvement of crystallinity [39]. It can be clearly seen that the number of diffraction peaks increases gradually from Ni-MOF 1–1 to Ni-MOF 1–6 shown in Fig. 3, and the diffraction peaks become obvious sharp crystalline diffraction peaks instead of broad diffraction peaks. These XRD results of Ni-MOFs indicate that the relative crystallinity of NiMOFs is gradually improved according to the increasing content of Ni element from Ni-MOF 1–1 to Ni-MOF 1–6. The phenomenon observed in this work is similar to the results previously reported in MOFs synthesis [39–41].
The FT-IR and Raman spectra of Ni-MOFs are presented in Fig. 4a and b. The distinct band at $3432\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to the stretching vibration of water molecules, indicating that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules existed within Ni-MOFs structure [42]. The strong bands at $1570\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1384\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of the coordinated ACOO groups, respectively. Among of the spectra for Ni-MOF 1–6, Ni-MOF 1–4, Ni-MOF 1–2 and Ni-MOF 1–1, these Ni-MOFs exhibit almost the same peak position. The separated bands between the asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes indicate that the $-\mathtt{C O O^{-}}$ of trimesic acid is coordinated to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ via a bidentate ligand mode and the $(\mathsf{B D C})_{3}$ -Ni exhibits a polymeric structure. These evidences can further demonstrate that the obtained samples are a kind of Ni-MOFs structure [33,43]. Furthermore, as shown in Raman spectrum (Fig. 4b), the D band and G band of Ni-MOFs exist at $1448~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1585\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are in agreement with those of Ni-MOFs structures reported [36]. In order to investigate the chemical composition of Ni-MOFs, XPS characterization and ICP-OES were carried out and the corresponding results were presented in Fig. 5 and Table 1. It is very clearly that only signals from Ni, O, and C elements are detected in the survey spectrum shown in Fig. 5a. As shown in Fig. 5b, two major Ni 2p peaks indicates that the Ni $2{\tt p}^{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}^{1/2}$ are centered at around 871.0 and $853.3\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [44,45]. The two other peaks centered at binding energies of 877.2 and $859.1\mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}^{1/2}$ and Ni $2{\mathrm{p}}^{3/\bar{2}}$ satellites, respectively [46]. In addition, the increasing intensity of NiMOFs reveals the improvement of Ni content from Ni-MOF 1–1 to Ni-MOF 1–6, which is directly demonstrated by the change content of Ni element characterized by XPS and ICP-OES analysis of elements (Table 1), the Ni-MOF 1–6 exhibits highest content of Ni element (49.01 wt% of XPS analysis and $32.24\mathrm{wt\%}$ of ICP-OES) than other Ni-MOFs.
The thermal stability of the Ni-MOFs samples were investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) from $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Fig. 6). There are two different stages of weight loss appeared in the TGA curves. In the first stage, about $6.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ loss from $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ could be attributed to the suffering the loss of water molecules. The second abrupt weight loss were occurred from $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ which related to the decomposition of the organic frameworks [31]. As shown in Fig. 6, the NiMOFs exhibits different weight loss from Ni-MOF 1–6 to Ni-MOF 1–1, the weight loss increases from $53.84\%$ (Ni-MOF 1–6) to $59.92\%$ (Ni-MOF 1–4), $62.45\%$ (Ni-MOF 1–2) and $66.32\%$ (Ni-MOF 1–1), respectively. These evidences further prove that the coordinated or crystallized $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules existence in the Ni-MOFs, which is in accordance with the FT-IR and Raman data. It also demonstrates that the increase content of organic frameworks affects the formation of Ni-MOFs structure, which results in conversion from the porous structure (Ni-MOF 1–6) to spherical structure (Ni-MOF 1–1). In addition, the specific surface area and pore size of Ni-MOFs were characterized by BET measurement, and the results were presented in Fig. 7a,b and Table 2. The specific surface area of hierarchical porous Ni-MOF is $40.36\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , whereas the pore size distribution curve obtained from the BJH method reveals that pore size of hierarchical porous Ni-MOF is $19.03\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is contributed by the nanosheets stacked. However, the specific surface area of Ni-MOFs exhibits obvious downward trend shown in Fig. 7a and Table 2, the values decrease from $40.36\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ to $28.28\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–4), $23.36\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–2), and 5.24 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–1), respectively. Obviously, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF structure exhibits highest specific surface area. Such high specific surface area and nanosheets stacked structure can provide more electrochemical reaction sites, which can improve the capacitive property as the electroactive materials.
The electrochemical performance of Ni-MOFs was investigated using a three electrode system in $3\mathrm{{M}}$ KOH electrolyte and the results were presented in Fig. 8a–f. As shown in Fig. 8a, the cyclic voltammetry (CV) was carried out with a potential range from $-0.1$ to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ at scan rates from $5\mathrm{mV}/s$ to $50\mathrm{mV}/s$ . A couple of distinct redox peaks were observable, indicating that the typical pseudocapacitive behavior caused by the surface faradic redox reactions, which were correspond to the reversible intercalation and deintercalation of $\mathrm{\o{OH^{-}}}$ ions [33,47,48]. This phenomena of CV curves agree well with the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves (Fig. 8b) under different current densities in a potential range of $_{0-0.4\mathrm{V}}$ . Obviously, there is a plateau in the potential from 0.15 to $0.3\mathrm{V}$ at different current densities from $1\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ to $30\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ Such a plateau is attributed to the redox reaction, and indicating the significant contribution of the pseudo-capacitance.
The electrochemical properties comparison of Ni-MOF 1–6, NiMOF 1–4, Ni-MOF 1–2 and Ni-MOF 1–1 were investigated with CV curves (Fig. 8c) at a scan rate of $20\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{s}$ and galvanostatic chargedischarge curves (Fig. 8d) at a current density of $1\:\mathsf{A}/\:\mathsf{g}.$ . Obviously, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF exhibits largest average area of a CV curve, and also reveals longest discharge time, which is correspond to the highest specific capacitance. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF exhibits optimal electrochemical performance, suggesting the merit of the elaborate architecture of Ni-MOF structure. The specific capacitance $C_{s}$ $(\mathbb{F}/\mathbb{g})$ is calculated from the galvanostatic charge-discharge measurement (Fig. 8d and Fig. S) according to the following equation [30,49]:
$$
C s=\frac{2I\int^{V}d t}{\left.V^{2}\right|_{V_{i}}^{V_{f}}}
$$
where $I$ is the discharge current density calculated using $I=i/m,$ i is the current and $m$ is the active mass of the single electrode, $\textstyle\int V d t$ is the integral current area of the discharge curve, $V$ is the potential with initial values $(V_{i})$ and final values $(V_{f})$ , respectively. The calculated $C_{s}$ values of Ni-MOFs are present in Fig. 8e. Obviously, it can be seen that the $C_{s}$ is in the order of Ni-MOF $1{-}6>\mathsf{N i-M O F}$ $1{-}4>\mathsf{N i}-$ - MOF $1{-}2>\mathsf{N i-M O F}$ 1–1, respectively. The hierarchical porous NiMOF exhibits excellent electrochemical performance, and possesses high capacitance of $1057.2\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ at $1\:\mathrm{A/g}$ , also delivers $649.8\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ at current density of $30\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ , which exhibits good rate capability of $61.5\%$ even up to high current density of $30\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . The Ni-MOFs can only reveal specific capacitance of $828.2\:\mathrm{F/g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–4), 581.2 $\mathbb{F}/\mathbb{g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–2) and $554.3\mathrm{F/g}$ (Ni-MOF 1–1), respectively, and retain $58.1\%$ , 57.4 and $49.1\%$ compared to their initial values of 1 $\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ , respectively. Comparison with other Ni-MOFs, the hierarchical porous structure exhibits better ion diffusion and electron transport ability. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF exhibits excellent electrochemical performance, which is comparable or higher than recently reported MOF electrode materials (Table 3).
The Nyquist plots of various Ni-MOFs are present in Fig. 8f. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement was characterized at a frequency range from $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ to $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ and used to evaluate the different structure of Ni-MOFs. Equivalent electrical circuit model was used for EIS data fitting (Fig. S2). All the parameters of equivalent circuit elements are summarized in Table S1. Among of various Ni-MOFs, the semicircles for the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF in the high frequency ranges are smallest than that of the other Ni-MOFs. A smaller semicircle reveals a smaller charge transfer resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ , and also indicates smaller internal resistance of electrode material, whereas the $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ for the Ni-MOF 1–6, Ni-MOF 1–4, MOF 1–2 and Ni-MOF 1–1 are $1.371\Omega.$ , $1.738\Omega$ , $9.717\Omega$ , and ${\displaystyle12.93~}\Omega$ , respectively. Above all, the lowest $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ is related to the morphology of electrode materials, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF 1–6 exhibits smaller size, which can shorten the electron transfer path. In addition, the hierarchical porous structure facilitates faster charge transport, which minimizes the interfacial resistance of the charge transfer process. The equivalent series resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{s}})$ was obtained from the intercept of the real axis in the high frequency range of the Nyquist plots, which contained series resistance of the electrolyte, electrode, electrolyte and electrode contact resistance. The $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ of the MOF 1–6, Ni-MOF 1–4, MOF 1–2 and Ni-MOF 1–1 are $1.141\Omega$ , $1.124\Omega$ , $1.188\Omega,$ and $1.243\Omega$ , respectively. Obviously, the incremental $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ reveals that the increase of nickel content can decrease internal resistance of electrode material, and realize smallest $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ . The lower $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ indicates that the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF exhibits high electrical conductivity.
The cycle stability and performance durability of hierarchical porous Ni-MOF were characterized using the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves at a current density of $10\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ and the results were plotted in Fig. 9. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF can maintain $70\%$ of its original capacitance after 2 500 cycles.
The ASCs were assembled according to the schematic illustration (Fig. 10a), which were constructed with Ni-MOF 1–6 as positive electrode and activated carbon (AC) as negative electrode. For assembling ASCs, two different types of electrodes were pressed together under face-to-face structure with the cellulose separator (NKK) caught in the middle, and 3 M KOH was used as the electrolyte. To further evaluate the electrochemical properties and estimate the stable potential windows of Ni-MOF 1–6 and AC, the CV curves of these two type electrode materials were carried out in 3 M KOH aqueous solution. The Ni-MOF 1–6 electrode was measured with a potential window from $-0.1$ to $0.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}}$ , while AC was evaluated within a potential window from $-1.0$ to $_{0\vee}$ at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}/s$ (Fig. 10b). Furthermore, the electrochemical behavior of ASCs was evaluated by CV and galvanostatic chargedischarge tests. CV curves of ASCs were investigated from 1.0 to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ with a scan rate at $50\mathrm{mV}/s$ (Fig. 10c), the CV curves could demonstrate that ASCs owned a high potential as high as $1.4{\sf V}.$ As shown in Fig. 10d, the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves were performed from 1.0 to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ at a current of $4\mathrm{mA}$ , which also proved that the ASCs could reach to a high potential of $1.4\mathrm{V}.$ Furthermore, the ASCs were investigated by CV test with a large potential window from 0 to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ at a different scan rates from 5 $\mathrm{mV}/s$ to $100\mathrm{mV}/s$ (Fig. 10e), and which also were measured by galvanostatic charge-discharge test from 0 to $1.4\mathrm{V}$ at different current densities from 0.5 to $10\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ (Fig. 10f), and these results also revealed that the ASCs could successfully reach a high potential at $1.4{\mathrm{V}}.$ In addition, the $C_{s}$ was calculated based on galvanostatic charge–discharge curves, and the $C_{s}$ value of ASCs was presented in Fig. $10{\mathrm{g}}$ . The ASCs exhibited specific capacitance of $87\ \mathrm{F/g}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ , and the $C_{s}$ could maintain $63\mathrm{F/g}$ at a current density of $10\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ .
The Ragone plot of as-fabricated ASCs, describing the relationship between energy density (E) and power density $(P)$ , is calculated based on the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves (Fig. 10 h). The $E$ can be calculated by $E=1/2C_{s}\Delta V^{2}$ , where $C_{s}$ is the specific capacitance, and $\Delta V$ is the potential change during the discharge process after voltage drop, respectively. And the $P$ can be calculated by $P=E/t$ , where $E$ is the energy density and the t is the discharge time of one cycles during the discharge process. The ASCs possesses a high $E$ of $21.05\mathrm{Wh/kg}$ at $P$ of $0.44\mathrm{~kW}/\mathrm{kg}$ and reveals a high $P$ of $6.03\mathrm{~kW}/\mathrm{kg}$ at E of $5.36\mathrm{Wh/kg}$ . This value is comparable or higher than those of the recently literatures on supercapacitors [29,50–55] (Fig. $10\mathrm{{h}}^{\cdot}$ ).
Furthermore, the cycle ability and performance durability of the ASCs were investigated at a current density of $5\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{g}$ as shown in Fig. 11. The capacitances maintained about $70\%$ of its initial value after 2000 charge/discharge cycles. Additionally, the ASCs based on Ni-MOF 1–6 and AC were further explored potential application, two ASCs units were connected to make a tandem device. The CV and galvanostatic charge-discharge curves of single ASCs and tandem devices were measurements and presented in Fig. 12a and b. It is clearly that the potential window is extended from $1.4\mathrm{V}$ of single ASCs to $2.8\mathrm{V}$ for two ASCs in series. Furthermore, the tandem SCs were charged at $2.8\mathrm{V}$ , and used to power a red LED. As shown in Fig. 12c, two ASCs in series connection can drive a red LED. Therefore, such hierarchical porous Ni-MOF structure can exhibit an excellent electrochemical performance and reveals a huge advantage as the electrode for high performance supercapacitors.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, novel hierarchical porous Ni-MOF was fabricated by a facile hydrothermal process with the existence of trimesic acid and nickel ions. The hierarchical Ni-MOF exhibits optimal structure, which exhibits largest specific surface area. The hierarchical porous Ni-MOF also possesses an outstanding specific capacitance of $1057\mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $1\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ which also exhibits good rate capability of $63.4\%$ $\left(649\mathrm{F/g}\right)$ even up to $30\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . Furthermore, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF keeps $70\%$ of its original value up to 2 500 charge-discharge cycles at the current density of $10\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . Furthermore, ASCs are assembled based on Ni-MOF 1–6 and AC, the ASCs reveal specific capacitance of $87\ \mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $0.5\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g},$ and exhibit high energy density of $21.05\mathrm{Wh/kg}$ and power density of $6.03\mathrm{~kW}/\mathrm{kg}$ . Additionally, the tandem ASCs can light up a red LED. Thus, the hierarchical porous Ni-MOF exhibits promising applications in high performance supercapacitors.
